e shortages for  instance around the air traffic control  that causes delays that could lead to  impact also clearly on our customers and  of course in the worst case also  cancellations but i think from the  actions that we have taken we are now  seeing that that is working and we  gather a program that is now stabilized  very much in line with the 2019 levels  of operation  what do you say to those people who  accuse you of having laid off too many  people during the pandemic and  that you're now struggling to recruit  but first of all it wasn't really an  option was it i mean we we had the  toughest restrictions in in across the  whole of the europe but we didn't know  when this restriction was going to be  lifted remember that the last  restrictions were lifted here in the uk  only as late as as in march and even by  the early part of of the this calendar  year you know the the government  couldn't say anything about the the  removal of these restrictions being in  place  so that wasn't simply an option but we  have recruited more people than we ever  done before in order to make sure that  we can deliver the summer and you know  we did have challenges and for that you  know we deeply regret that if that has  affected casper and it has to some  extent but i must also then say that you  know since the actions we have taken  here seems to pretty much you know  throughout the the queen's jubilee week  and onwards we have delivered a stable  program we're flying  you know some 250 000 customers per day  with the the same performance you would  expect to see in in a normal year  what about industrial relations you've  got a few strikes going on around the  business uh in in coming weeks do you  have a problem on that front  well i think to the extent that we have  had those issues as well i think we've  been you know very good in mitigating  that in terms of planning around that  and we continue to work you know  constructively with our union partners  in order to make sure that we can get  resolutions on these things and this is  something that is not you know clearly  unique for one airline that exists  freely across the whole of the ecosystem  as well and for that matter not only  within this industry so that's something  that we're going to continue to work  focus on together with our partners  now during the pandemic you stopped  flying out of newcastle south end and  stansted i noticed that you're you're  back to an extent at some of those sites  now was it a mistake to pull out from  those  no i think that that is something that  that that we did because we had to  restructure the network and what we did  was also down to to remove unprofitable  flying that we had and we weren't happy  with that performance because at the  same time we also saw that we had bigger  demand elsewhere that we wanted to  fulfill as part of that so that is part  of kind of the ongoing way on how we  optimize  the network to really meet the customer  demand that we have across the  whole of our operations  now you're the biggest carrier operating  at gatwick how unhelpful is it that  they've capped passenger numbers in the  way they have  well i i i think that i might be a  little bit different to some of the  other airline ceos of this i think that  you know it is better to have an airport  who are assessing the capacity levels  and the resilience level across the  whole of the the network the ground  handlers and the security staff and what  they actually think that they are  capable of delivering and if they feel  that they can't do that and they obsess  that that is the case it's better to  give a heads up on that so we can plan  our programs accordingly i mean it's not  great and disappointing to the effect  that that has for the customers but you  rather do that don't you rather than to  have customers coming out to the airport  and then suddenly realize that the  flight discounts is on the day because  that is the worst thing that that that  you really want to avoid so you know we  we've been supportive of what gatwick  has been doing in terms of those  capacity costs because if they just  don't think that they can operate it we  shouldn't risk the customers being  stranded there on on the day  now obviously you're seeing a lot of  pent-up demand in the business right now  i just wonder how how it is specifically  in business travel obviously leisure is  pretty buoyant what about business  travel  we definitely see that that is is coming  back as well we do see that those routes  that we have you know you know a high  proportion of business travel is being  being in place i think we're now down to  you know what would be a normal a lower  level of period as well because it's  it's in the middle of the summer so that  business travel hasn't you know come  back to to to to those levels that would  seem but that follows a kind of a normal  pattern in there but i think in general  the trends on what people are booking  and not booking you can say that we're  strong into the mediterranean and that  does really well we've seen some  softness into the domestic flying in  certain cases uh we we do see that for  instance from a holiday the people tend  to gravitate towards the all-inclusive  uh proposition because it probably gives  you know people then you know the  security of what the cost is going to be  on on that holiday  but it's really the traditional beach  and leicester routes that is doing well  for us  all right good to talk to you this  morning thanks for joining me  thank you
 

